Secure Code Review of a Login System
Overview

This experiment involves a manual security review of a PHP-based login and registration system to identify insecure coding practices and propose improvements based on secure coding standards.

Objectives
Identify vulnerabilities in authentication systems
Analyze insecure coding practices
Review database interaction security
Suggest secure coding improvements

Tools Used
PHP
MySQL
XAMPP Server
VS Code
Web Browser

System Description

The system consists of:

Login module (index.php)
Registration module (register.php)
MySQL database (login_system)

Security Review Findings
⚠️ SQL Injection Risk

User inputs directly used in SQL queries without sanitization.

⚠️ Plain-Text Password Storage

Passwords stored without hashing or encryption.

⚠️ Missing Input Validation

No validation for username/password fields.

⚠️ Debug Mode Enabled

Error messages exposed to users.

⚠️ No Session Management

No authentication session handling after login.

Recommended Improvements
Use prepared statements (PDO / MySQLi)
Implement bcrypt password hashing
Add input validation and sanitization
Disable debug output in production
Implement session-based authentication

Outcome

The system was found to contain multiple critical security vulnerabilities that could lead to unauthorized access and data compromise. Secure coding practices significantly improve system security.

Conclusion

Secure code review is essential in identifying vulnerabilities early in development. Proper authentication design prevents major security risks such as SQL injection and credential leakage.